  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 317 AM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nThe threat for strong to severe storms persists into Thursday, \nprimarily for areas near and east of HWY 83. A surface trough is \nexpected to bisect the area on Thursday afternoon, providing a focus \nfor convective development. Flow aloft remains strong enough to \nsupport a supercellular storm mode, and with ample instability in \nplace suggests a threat for both large hail and damaging winds. \n\nUpper ridging then begins to establish establish across the \nRockies and amplifies into the weekend. This brings height rises\naloft along with increasing warm advection. Highs return to the\nupper 80s to lower 90s for late week and this weekend, \nreturning to near and slightly above average for mid-August. \nThis increasing subsidence aloft also casts some doubt on \nthunderstorm threats, though confidence in this remains low for \nnow. With the eventual approach of this trough axis, lesser \nwinds aloft will lead to weaker deep layer shear and at least \nsome reprieve in the recent severe weather across the area.",
